WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:09.190
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:10.451
On behalf of

00:00:10.451 --> 00:00:13.857
The Council on Latin American
and Iberian Studies (CLAIS),

00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:19.690
let me welcome, especially
on behalf of her director,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.670
Claudia Valeggia and her
team, she couldn't be here.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:26.360
And especially to this fourth event

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.610
of the Latin American
Policy Leader Series.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.270
My name is Jessica Faieta.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.560
I am a Senior Fellow at

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.950
the Jackson Institute of Global Affairs

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.270
and lecturer on

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.400
Development Issues in Latin
America and the Caribbean.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.120
And today I have the distinct pleasure

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.900
and honor to welcome our
speaker María-Noel Vaeza

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.540
the Regional Director for
Latin America and Caribbean

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.760
in a very important institution
of the United Nations,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.853
UN Women.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.610
It is a particular pleasure
because María-Noel and I

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.020
are colleagues from the United
Nations for the longest time.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.710
I have been in the past
before coming to Yale,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.820
30 years in the United Nations
and a big part of this,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.970
we have been colleagues with, and friends,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.083
good friends with María-Noel.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.520
And both we have worked in
Latin American the Caribbean.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.180
We have coincided in many ways,

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.610
most recently when I was

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.150
Assistant Secretary-General

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.640
and Regional Director in the region.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.570
So I can attest the firsthand that,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.120
of her knowledge about
the region, her expertise,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.870
but most importantly, her commitment to,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.880
in this case, to the advancement of women.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.880
So she is really fantastic
guest for this policy series.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:58.830
The title of her talk is,
"Diversity, Equality and Gender."

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.200
And I think you will agree with me

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.140
that this subject is particularly relevant

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.210
to reflect on this, not only
in Latin America and Caribbean,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.130
but I think even in the country

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.347
that we are right now holding this.

00:02:14.347 --> 00:02:17.560
And of course in Yale
University where this subject

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.180
is also of great interest
to the students and faculty.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.710
We hope that today's
event will provide a forum

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:28.757
for discussing the role of
organizations such as UN Women

00:02:28.757 --> 00:02:33.757
to, for the advancement
of diversity and gender.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.450
The Latin American Policy Leaders Series

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.340
is organized by CLAIS,
seeks to create dialogue

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.660
about the future of democratization,

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.800
human rights, and development,
with policy leaders

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.490
from across Latin
America in the Caribbean.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.070
With this initiative,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.010
CLAIS hopes to engage
leaders from the region

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.980
in vigorous and respectful discussions

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.439
about pressing issues,

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:04.030
while also benefiting from
their years of experience.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.084
So certainly it's an
honor for me to be invited

00:03:08.084 --> 00:03:12.970
to host and to moderate
the talk of Maria-Noel,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.610
as she possesses the
right kind of experience

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.950
that we need to hear right now.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.940
So let me tell you a little
bit about María-Noel.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.590
She assumed her functions

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.590
as Regional Director for the
Americas in the Caribbean,

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:32.170
in UN Women in 2019, but
before that, she worked as

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.795
the Director of the Program
Division at headquarters.

00:03:36.795 --> 00:03:41.795
And she joined from another
UN organization, UNOPS,

00:03:42.523 --> 00:03:44.100
where she served as

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.340
the Director for Global
Portfolio, and before that

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.250
Partnerships Practice and
in Strategic Partnerships.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.930
So she has a long and distinguished career

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.010
in the United Nations.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.550
She also spent eight years in UNDP,

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:02.230
where she held various posts,
including a Senior Advisor,

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.560
Deputy Resident
Representative in Paraguay,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.600
and Senior Manager

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.203
for the Recovery, Reconciliation
and Reform Program.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.150
She also has a distinguished career

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.710
in public service in her own country

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.130
because prior to joining
the United Nations,

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.910
María-Noel held multiple positions in

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:24.340
Uruguay's Ministry of Foreign Affairs,

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.983
including as Political Counselor

00:04:26.983 --> 00:04:29.730
at Uruguay's Embassy to the United States,

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:34.510
and as Delegate of Uruguay
to the UN General Assembly.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.970
She also was an alternate
Representative of Uruguay

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.323
to the World Bank Board of Directors.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.240
María-Noel is a national from Uruguay.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.760
She holds a Doctorate of
Law and Social Sciences

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.150
from University of the
Republic of Uruguay,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.580
and she also has a Master's Degree

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.590
in International and Public Policy

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.653
from Johns Hopkins
University in Washington, DC.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.330
The structure of today

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.390
is something you have
already are familiar with.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.533
She will have her talk.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.130
We will then follow with
some moderated discussion,

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.490
which you are invited to raise your hand.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.900
And I am particularly
welcoming all the people,

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.070
all the students and faculty
who are, and public in general,

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:26.673
who are joining us right now on online.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.490
Before turning the
microphone to our speaker,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.910
I would like to thank the
sponsors of this event.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:37.460
The Latin American Policy Leader Series

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.330
is generously supported by the

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.830
George Herbert Walker Jr.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.001
Lecture Fund in International Studies

00:05:44.001 --> 00:05:47.560
at the McMillan Center
for International Studies.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.202
And this event would
not have been possible

00:05:49.202 --> 00:05:52.100
without the assistance
of the collaborators,

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.930
such as the Office of
International Affairs,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.680
the Fox International Fellowship Program,

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.120
and the Program for Peace and Development,

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:01.610
and if I may also add,

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:06.610
the Jackson Institute for Global
Affairs, to which I belong.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.630
Thank you to the staff and the colleagues

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.130
who made this visit possible,
and without further ado,

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.851
María-Noel, you have the floor.

00:06:14.851 --> 00:06:17.851
(audience applauds)

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.202
- I feel so old every time I hear my CV,

00:06:25.202 --> 00:06:26.035
(general laughter)

00:06:26.035 --> 00:06:28.750
It means that I've been
around for some time.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:29.840
Thank you, Jessica.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.470
It's a real pleasure and honor to be here

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.150
and also being introduced

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.750
by such a distinguished
colleague as Jessica.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.770
We've known each other
for a long time as well,

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.620
but Jessica has been a
leader in our region.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.450
And also we are very happy to see

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:49.230
that that she's here in Yale.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.760
I think that we practitioners,

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.378
we have something to say and to transmit,

00:06:54.378 --> 00:06:59.378
but it not always happens
that we have this opportunity.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.430
So I'm really grateful for
Yale and for the faculty,

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:05.530
for all the colleagues

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.480
that has been so kind with
me since I arrived yesterday.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.449
So thank you so much for
giving me this opportunity.

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:18.029
For me, it's very important
to bring to your attention,

00:07:18.029 --> 00:07:19.900
what is UN Women?

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.420
What are we doing?

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.850
and why it's important to
focus on women's issues.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.010
And women in all their diversity,

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:33.220
because we are not, you
know, the same women.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.950
That's why the concept
of intersectionality

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.570
is so dear to UN Women.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:41.040
So I would like to start by saying,

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.040
what do we do in, well,
I dunno, it doesn't work,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.620
but I would like to, to share with you,

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.309
what is the situation of equality,

00:07:52.309 --> 00:07:57.140
of gender equality, and on
gender inequality as well.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:00.260
But what are the key areas of UN Women?

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.220
Where do we work in the region

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.160
and how do we see gender
inequality in our region?

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.300
And what are the solutions
that we are proposing?

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.540
Because we try to focus in
certain solutions before,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.770
you know, instead of being everywhere,

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.080
we try to focus and try to
move the needle as we say,

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.900
and try to change the reality of women.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.350
So we do three things,

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:31.450
basically in our mandate
from the general assembly,

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:33.900
we were created 11 years ago.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.133
We are really young for the UN.

00:08:37.218 --> 00:08:40.980
And we were created as a
merge of several institutions

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:45.000
that were dealing bits
and pieces of gender.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.340
And it was a very intelligent decision,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.490
I must say, from the general assembly,

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.200
because we could really
merge the specialization

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:56.610
that were in the UN everywhere.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.480
And we had the opportunity to have

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.010
our first Executive Director
was Madam Michelle Bachelet,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.520
the former President of Chile

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.120
and right now she is the High
Commissioner for Human Rights.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.570
So it was a real honor
to have her with us.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:13.860
I was not in UN Women at that time,

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:16.870
but I was envious of
the UN Women colleagues

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.630
because they had such a
distinguished executive director.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:23.410
And we decided at that time

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.250
that we should focus our attention

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.730
on women political participation,

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.970
women in the public space,

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:34.320
on women economic empowerment,
the autonomy of women,

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.320
because we really are half of
the population of the world,

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.210
but we are not contributing to the economy

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:43.870
as half the population of the world,

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:47.030
and that's why the world
is not growing enough,

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:49.470
because we are not enough participating.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.500
And of course, violence against women,

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.989
which is the biggest, I would say,

00:09:53.989 --> 00:09:57.060
shadow pandemic that we have

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.600
that came more in light
during the pandemic,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.530
because it was obvious
that this was a big issue

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.350
that everybody needed to attend.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:09.690
And of course we are
universal. We are global.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.560
So women peace and security

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.660
is a very important part of our work

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:17.718
and the humanitarian
response of what we do

00:10:17.718 --> 00:10:19.440
in that parts of the world.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.630
But, you know, I didn't know
when I came to Latin America,

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:26.210
when I came back to Latin America,

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:31.210
that the region was going to
be in such enormous problems.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:34.220
I thought it was a nice position to be,

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:36.860
to really establish innovation,

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:40.450
to try to work differently
from other UN agencies

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:43.520
and, you know, calmly integrate our work

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.310
and gender perspective in the work

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:46.960
of the other UN agencies.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.790
And suddenly we have
social unrest everywhere.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:52.340
It was Chile, it was Bolivia,

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:56.150
it was Ecuador, it was
Colombia, it was everywhere.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:00.770
And it was a really a boost of energy

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:02.690
coming from the women's movement

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:06.550
because it started on the 8th
of March in Santiago, Chile,

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:10.170
and then in continue to uprising

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.510
in the indigenous population in Ecuador,

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.430
but it was women's movement,

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.000
indigenous women's
movement that were behind.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.858
And of course, then we have it in Colombia

00:11:19.858 --> 00:11:23.660
and Bolivia was in chaos
because of the coup-d'etat,

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.300
so it was everywhere.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.170
And I said, "What is
going on in my region?"

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:33.130
I thought we are overcoming
the governance issues."

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:37.070
But it wasn't and it didn't,
and it's a big question mark.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.400
What is gonna happen with our governance?

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.930
What is gonna happen with gender equality?

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.150
We have only one president
who declare himself feminist,

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:47.660
which is Boric, the President of Chile,

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.620
but rapidly he's losing popularity.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.560
And so he doesn't even have a hundred days

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.860
and he's already been a pariah

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.890
in many aspects in his country.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:01.110
And we have the
constitutional reform in Chile

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.310
that is also a beacon of hope,

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.228
how the women's issues are gonna be,

00:12:06.228 --> 00:12:08.100
you know, in the constitution.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:12.450
So here we are trying to attend all this

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.980
and suddenly the pandemic came,

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.910
but we knew that 74%

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:21.940
of who was in the front lines
of the pandemic were women,

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:23.990
but only 20% of women

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:26.880
were at the forefront
of the decision making.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.880
So there you start seeing
the gap, the discrimination,

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:37.170
and the lack of understanding
that women have also a say,

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:38.440
and they have a say

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:41.670
that perhaps is innovative, is different.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:46.140
You can only look at how the leaders,

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:49.323
11% of the leaders of the world are women.

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:52.440
And if you see those countries,

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.810
those countries managed
the pandemic very well.

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.870
Like the prime minister of New Zealand,

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:00.580
the chancellor of Germany,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.210
the prime minister of Norway, of Finland.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:05.700
So there is a common thread

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:10.330
that women perhaps has a
different way of leadership,

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:13.410
of making sure that everybody

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:16.840
is in the table before taking decisions,

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:21.700
listening more, and of course
we don't know everything,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.120
so we need to consult.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:25.750
So perhaps this kind of leadership

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.390
with these soft skills that
we call them, you know,

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:31.560
is what the world needs right now

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:35.620
to really confront several
crisis at the same time,

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:39.350
like the pandemic brought the into light.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.610
So for us in Latin America,

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:47.610
it has been a horrible consequence,
the pandemic for women.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.670
We were a region

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:54.030
that was growing the participation
of women in the economy

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:58.470
and the pandemic stopped
that, very much so,

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:02.640
and now we went back 18 years,

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:07.640
we went back 18 years because
of one reason, which is care.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:12.650
We women are the main care providers.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:17.560
And suddenly the world realized
that care is very important

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:21.160
because most of the COVID patients

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:25.270
were at home being care by whom, by women.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:30.270
So care came to the front
lines and care came as saying,

00:14:32.527 --> 00:14:34.520
"This is an unpaid care work

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:39.180
that we do more than everybody else."

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.730
So we have weeks of eight days

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.710
because our work is to care

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:49.350
and our work without recognition,

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:51.480
without redistribution,

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.930
and without reduction
of the unpaid care work.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.910
And unfortunately, although we thought

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:01.350
this was going to be changed
in the pandemic, it didn't.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:04.706
Women continued to do
three times more care,

00:15:04.706 --> 00:15:08.100
unpaid care work than men.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:12.800
And in some countries, we
did surveys like in Chile,

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:16.080
it increased the unpaid care work of men,

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.220
seven minutes, seven minutes!

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:21.140
When we had the children at home,

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:24.780
when we had to be there
for them for the education,

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:27.570
when we had to take care of elderly,

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.840
take care of the children,

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.700
it means that men are
not being co-responsible

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:36.740
of the household, of the
education of children,

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.800
of taking care of their own elderly,

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:41.550
because normally women take
care of their own elderly

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.850
and the elderly of their partners.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:48.850
So unfortunately this requires
the attention, not just from,

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:52.490
from us as UN Women, the UN system,

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:54.350
but also from civil society,

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:58.070
from governments, and particular
from the social media.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.016
I think the power of the
social media right now

00:16:01.016 --> 00:16:06.000
give us the opportunity to
really change this behavior of

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:11.000
and understand that without
the co-responsibility of men

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:14.380
in the household it's impossible for women

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.700
to really thrive and participate.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:18.060
And that's why women

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:22.430
are not searching actively
for jobs in my region,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:25.900
because they have to stay
home and provide care.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.340
This is why my number one priority

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.030
with all my colleagues in the region,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:34.700
we are 550 gender experts in the region,

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:39.320
in 26 countries, so we
are quite a crowd there.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.300
And we decided to focus our attention

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:46.170
on systems of care, the society of care.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:50.170
We will have the Regional
Conference of Women in Argentina,

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:53.330
on the first week of November,
second week of November,

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:57.330
we have this regional conference
for the last 45 years,

00:16:57.330 --> 00:17:02.180
is in the family of ECLAC, is
intergovernmental conference.

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:05.470
The first one was in Havana 45 years ago

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.710
so it means that this is an institution

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:09.820
that we need to preserve.

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:12.450
And the subject is Society of Care,

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:13.840
not just the care of people,

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:17.420
but the care of biodiversity,
the care of climate.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:21.677
So for us establishing
integrated system of care

00:17:21.677 --> 00:17:26.340
that integrate not just
the federal levels,

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:29.520
the sub-national levels,
the municipal levels,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.523
but private and public,

00:17:31.523 --> 00:17:34.969
and also community level
because indigenous population,

00:17:34.969 --> 00:17:37.510
they do care in a different manner

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:39.000
that we need to integrate.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.140
So that's what we are working.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.130
We are working 12 countries

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:47.130
to establish the legal framework,
to establish the costing.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:52.250
How much it cost to the government
to finance a care system.

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.300
That it is an investment

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.470
because we always say that
it has three dividends.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:02.080
The first dividend is
the care to the family,

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:03.980
the welfare of the family

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:07.240
because when you have an
elderly, a sick person,

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:10.790
a disabled, kids, very small kids,

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:13.462
when you have professional support,

00:18:13.462 --> 00:18:16.800
everybody will be happier
and particularly the woman

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:20.000
that is doing the care, unpaid care.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.200
The second is for the economy,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.200
because there is a creation of jobs

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:28.060
that is very important
and is a green economy,

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:31.010
is, they call it the orange economy

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:33.010
because it's not polluting,

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:35.060
but you are giving the opportunity

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:38.410
to those women that are
working in the informal sector,

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:42.690
normally as domestic workers,

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.420
you have to give them
the opportunity to study,

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:49.290
to do a certification,
and to really go out there

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:52.230
and be caregivers, more professional.

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:55.470
So we reckon that this will
create millions of jobs,

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.520
and it will dignify

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:00.320
the women that are working
in informal sector,

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:02.100
that don't have benefits,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.575
that don't have anything
except the little salary

00:19:05.575 --> 00:19:08.750
they receive, when they receive it.

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.300
Because in the pandemic, we saw that they,

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:12.620
some of them were slaves.

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:14.960
They were not allowed to leave the houses

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:16.300
because of the contagion.

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:20.740
And we believe that those
jobs will be paying for taxes.

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:25.700
So the system that we are
trying to create everywhere

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:27.510
will pay by itself.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.270
There is an initial investment,

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:30.910
but then it will be paid by itself

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:33.820
by the creation of jobs
in the care economy.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:36.710
And the third dividend is for women

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:41.060
to have the opportunity
to go out and work.

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:44.650
And of course, get out of
poverty, because the moment

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:48.030
the women have subsidies to for care,

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:52.590
or the women can provide
proper care for their own,

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:53.960
they, she goes to work

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:57.330
and of course she will invest
back in their own family.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.039
So for us, that's a priority,

00:19:59.039 --> 00:20:02.150
together with financial inclusion.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:06.020
So we will, we are trying
as well to make sure

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:10.397
that the different
investors, banks, you know,

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:18.760
crowd funds, venture capital,
is you name it, fin techs,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:23.520
everybody, have a special
consideration of women

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:28.520
because unfortunately we
are only 11% of targets.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:30.840
So this is not possible

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:35.840
that 89% of the funds
are channeled to men.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:38.080
We need to change that reality

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:41.180
and for that, we open up a platform.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:44.129
And what we are trying to
do is to bring everybody

00:20:44.129 --> 00:20:48.900
and we call it the Platform
for in Gender Investments.

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:52.600
And there are three things that
for me are essential there.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:56.190
One is, those are investments

00:20:56.190 --> 00:20:59.630
that are gonna yield very
positive desire, the results.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:02.710
You're gonna make money
by investing in women.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:05.690
And also you are given the opportunity

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:09.140
to those companies or entrepreneurs,

00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:11.510
that are trying to get out of,

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:15.870
of their micro-enterprise
learning, lending

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:20.080
that is all well and good, but
you never get out of poverty,

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:24.920
to scale up their entrepreneurs,
entrepreneur objectives,

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:27.720
and really, you know,

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:30.110
provide this opportunity
to women to thrive.

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:33.290
And the third for me, which is essential,

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:37.430
is the impact of the social investment,

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:40.250
because you are not
only investing in women,

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:43.900
you're investing in the community
where women is uncertain.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.200
And for that, we have seven
principles that we call it,

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:50.370
the Women Economic Empowerment Principles,

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:52.820
that help the private sector,

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:55.840
and the private banks,
and the private capitals,

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:58.970
to understand that this is
not just one investment;

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.842
this is an overarching framework

00:22:02.842 --> 00:22:05.910
to be successful for an investment.

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:09.070
And then of course, women
political participation

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:10.610
is still for us very important.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:14.500
We have only 32% of women in parliament,

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:18.960
but we have very important
examples of parity in all,

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.620
like it was in the last
elections in Mexico.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:23.530
It was a political decision to say,

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:27.730
let's have election parity in everywhere:

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:31.550
governors, municipal
level, the parliament.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:33.834
And this is working very nicely,

00:22:33.834 --> 00:22:38.160
it's a lab of parity in the world.

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:41.740
And then Bolivia that
declare parity many years ago

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:45.470
and we still have 51%
of women in the Senate.

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:47.890
So this is also very nice.

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:50.000
And also Costa Rica, Argentina,

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.960
there are countries that they have worked

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:55.070
through different laws, you know,

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:57.530
how to get to parity or to quotas.

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.800
But the rest of the region we are still

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:02.880
quite low in women
political participation,

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:07.820
not just in the parliament,
but also as mayors.

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:11.740
We have only 15% of women
mayors in Latin America,

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:14.270
15% is nothing.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:16.650
Of course, we have Mexico City.

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:21.050
We have Bogota, which are
enormous cities, but anyway,

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:25.060
you know, we need to push
women more at the local level.

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.940
And for us also,

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:30.990
it is important too, actions
at the public sector,

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:34.100
which is the gender responsive budget.

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.350
The national budgets are still

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:38.680
the most important development instrument

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.240
that a country has,

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:42.100
but it doesn't have a gender lens.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:45.380
So we really need to work
different methodologies

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:49.950
to push for gender budgets,
and gender planning,

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:52.400
and gender statistics, et cetera.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:54.800
What you don't count, it doesn't exist.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:58.750
And most of the time women
are completely invisible

00:23:58.750 --> 00:23:59.920
and women of color

00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:03.873
or women of indigenous descent or LGTBI,

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:07.330
they are completely invisible.

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:08.840
So we need to work

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:11.883
on those statistics to
bring them to the light.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:16.190
Then women economic
empowerment, as I was saying,

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:19.100
the economy of care
and financial inclusion

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.270
are the things that we have prioritized

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:23.270
because we believe they need the most.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:25.210
And of course, violence against women.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:29.520
Violence against women is a
horrible problem that we have.

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:33.470
If you can think in Mexico,
there are 12 women that,

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:37.940
that dying a day, just for
the sake of being a woman.

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:41.239
So we really need to,
and we have more like

00:24:41.239 --> 00:24:46.239
4,000 feminicides a year in the region.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.700
So those are buried data

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:51.800
that we need to change the reality.

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:53.720
And how do we change that?

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:55.760
By changing legal frameworks,

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.010
by making sure that we have the data,

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:02.340
because it's very, you know,
it's very scattered the data.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:06.010
So we really need to,
and to have, you know,

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:10.840
centers of care, centers
that can care women

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:15.840
when they are first being
victims of violence, right?

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:20.040
So this is what we normally do.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.460
We work with the framework,
we work with data,

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:24.510
we work with integrated,

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:29.060
integrated different
inter-agencies response

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:31.124
that government has, et cetera.

00:25:31.124 --> 00:25:33.070
And then of course, for us,

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:37.300
it's essential to understand the financial

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:39.340
of those gender programs.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:43.050
And we are pushing for
STEMs, women in STEMs.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:45.500
We don't have enough
girls, or adolescents,

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:48.280
studies, the careers of the future,

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:50.273
or the career of the present,

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.280
so we need women in technology,

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:55.350
that's why we have several initiatives

00:25:55.350 --> 00:26:00.170
to introduce women to technology,
to science, et cetera.

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:03.980
We are still only 30% of
women that are studying

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:07.600
or working in the STEMs
area in the region.

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:12.227
So, and the last point
for me will be democracy,

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:15.530
governance, social unrest,

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:18.770
and all the things that
we have been watching

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:21.180
all around in the media,

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:25.270
but also the lack of opportunity for women

00:26:25.270 --> 00:26:29.230
to be in the decision making tables

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:33.230
and to bring their own perspective,

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:36.620
which is different from
men, but also the solutions

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:39.680
because women have very
important solutions.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:43.835
As mothers, as you
know, community leaders,

00:26:43.835 --> 00:26:46.660
they can see things in
a different perspective

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:50.010
and they can help
governments and civil society

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.771
and private sector to bring
a different perspective

00:26:53.771 --> 00:26:58.130
and a different human
development, if I may say.

00:26:58.130 --> 00:26:59.770
So I would like to stop there

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.680
and thank you very much for listening and,

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:05.460
and I'm here for answering any questions

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:08.270
and receiving recommendations as well,

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:09.492
but thank you so much.

00:27:09.492 --> 00:27:11.742
(applause)

00:27:16.614 --> 00:27:18.431
- Thank you, María-Noel,
can I invite you too?

00:27:18.431 --> 00:27:19.264
- Okay.

00:27:19.264 --> 00:27:22.563
- To sit here so we can
now have a conversation.

00:27:23.735 --> 00:27:24.972
- Ooh, is this comfortable!

00:27:24.972 --> 00:27:27.530
(general laughter)

00:27:27.530 --> 00:27:28.430
- Thank you very much.

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:30.853
You have given us really a,

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:36.100
a very sobering, I think, situation

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:39.420
on what is happening in Latin
America and the Caribbean

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:43.880
with regard to diversity,
to the situation of women.

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:47.430
But I also found it very encouraging

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:50.250
that you are working

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:53.880
and focusing on very specific solutions,

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.870
especially when you
talk about the systems,

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:02.530
integral systems of care,
and also empowering of women

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:06.170
with very clear solutions going forward.

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:08.830
Let me open for some questions

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:11.050
before I have some questions of my own,

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:14.850
but I'd like to give the
opportunity to the students

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:18.860
and also to the public who is connected

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:21.270
to give us some questions.

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:26.023
So anybody that has some
questions, please raise your hand.

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:35.800
Well, while they get, well,
the, oh, here we have, please.

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:38.790
Please introduce yourself,
from what school you are,

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:42.403
what year, and go ahead
with your question.

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:45.900
- My name is Daryl, I'm a
first year Master's student

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.926
at the Jackson Institute.

00:28:47.926 --> 00:28:50.244
I was wondering, I was wondering,

00:28:50.244 --> 00:28:54.040
were there any thoughts about paying

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.010
just women just for
being at home in general?

00:28:57.010 --> 00:28:59.820
So you mentioned having
care workers come in

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.040
and allow women to go out and work,

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.780
but paying women for the
work that they already do.

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:08.230
Was that like any policy
discussions around that or?

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:11.290
- Yes. Thank you for your question.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.240
And yes, indeed.

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:14.580
In my country Uruguay,

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:19.580
it exists sort of a payment
for staying home mothers.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:23.670
And it's a, it's a subsidy.

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:25.380
I wouldn't like to call it subsidy

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:30.380
because if you analyze the
time that women spend in care,

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:34.850
it could get to 20% of the GDP.

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:38.500
We did that study in
Mexico, and in Colombia,

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:43.220
and it's much more than oil
and minerals than everything.

00:29:43.220 --> 00:29:45.420
So it means that the
development of the country

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:46.840
is in the shoulders of women.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:49.991
So this is a payment that the government

00:29:49.991 --> 00:29:53.290
of Uruguay, Columbia,

00:29:53.290 --> 00:29:55.710
Panama, Argentina,

00:29:55.710 --> 00:30:00.280
they decided to do to women
to stay home and care.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:02.420
And it's working very nicely

00:30:02.420 --> 00:30:05.447
because some of the women are saying,

00:30:05.447 --> 00:30:08.700
"I'd rather stay home than be out there."

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:13.700
And what we notice is that we did a study

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:18.800
some years ago about the pyramid of women,

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:23.480
the women that are in
the bases of the pyramid,

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:26.340
women that were mothers,

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:31.340
because they were raped at 15 or 16 years,

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:33.100
they are very poor.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:37.090
They don't, they haven't
finished their education,

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:40.380
those women if they
want to go out for work,

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:45.380
they have to indebt themselves
to get a care provider.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:50.800
So we have internal debt of
the most poor of the women,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:53.720
because there isn't a system of care

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:57.420
that can allow them to go to work

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:01.860
and to get out of poverty, or
go back to study or et cetera.

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.950
So for us, this has been something

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:07.470
that we really brought into the attention.

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:10.130
There are some women
that want to stay home,

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.110
they need to have that payment,

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:15.580
but there are some women that
they want more from life,

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:17.570
they want to continue studying.

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:21.440
So we really, when we provide the,

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.300
I would say the technical
assistance, we put all this.

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:25.400
So we were discussing

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:28.310
with the Minister of a
Social Affairs of Chile

00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:30.100
on this respect,

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:34.470
to map how many women are,
how much this is gonna cost.

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:37.790
So we create a different
methodology of costing

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:40.980
of the different care,
you know, care provider,

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:44.890
the person that wants to stay
home, how much the payment,

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:48.350
but we should pay attention to

00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:53.350
the time that women uses
on a daily basis for care.

00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:55.083
And that has a value.

00:31:56.252 --> 00:32:00.610
And it's more valuable than
any industry in the country.

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:05.000
But yes, this is part of our toolkit

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:07.663
of advice to governments.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:11.960
- Please.

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:12.960
- Hi, my name is Brianna.

00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:14.380
I'm a graduating student

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:16.340
at the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:19.110
I'm also Mexican and I worked
last summer with ECLAC,

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:22.410
helping them plan their
original conference for women.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:25.730
My question for you is how
to foster political will

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:26.980
in the political climate

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:28.870
that is going on right
now in Latin America,

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:31.680
incentivize these
governments that right now

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:34.180
have so many needs to cover

00:32:34.180 --> 00:32:37.340
to make that initial investment in women,

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.630
how to convince president,

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:40.604
how to convince the ministers of finance,

00:32:40.604 --> 00:32:43.580
to listen to the ministers of the women

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:47.500
and invest money in care systems?

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:50.352
- Thank you for your
question, muchas gracias.

00:32:50.352 --> 00:32:53.540
Well, we are trying to convince them.

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:56.170
And last Sunday, the
president of Argentina,

00:32:56.170 --> 00:32:59.800
and Argentina is in a big,
big trouble economically,

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:02.850
he announced that he was signing the law

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:07.200
for creating the care system
and he put it as an investment.

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:08.663
Well, we did the talking points.

00:33:08.663 --> 00:33:09.970
(general laughter)

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:14.151
I can, I hope it doesn't
get out of here, but,

00:33:14.151 --> 00:33:15.310
(general laughter)

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:17.350
but it was a convincing,

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:19.350
not just from the minister of gender,

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:23.490
but also from different
arguments, economic arguments.

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:27.020
The meeting we had with
the minister of economy

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:32.020
was pure economic value of
investing in the care giving.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:37.257
And I remember his face,
he looked at us and said,

00:33:37.257 --> 00:33:40.770
"Okay, I will, I accept that."

00:33:40.770 --> 00:33:45.440
So when you bring numbers,
when you compare industries,

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:49.920
we did a study in Turkey
together with UNDP

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:51.740
and the University of Ankara

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:55.230
to compare the infrastructure
sector and the care system

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:58.470
that we wanted the ministry
of finance to invest.

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:01.223
And he was convinced
with the sheer numbers.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:07.160
And I guess that we people
who work in development

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:11.630
and with gender issues,
normally we are seen as,

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:15.866
you know, the crazy
feminists that go and march,

00:34:15.866 --> 00:34:17.150
and we are not that.

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:19.465
We are that, plus!

00:34:19.465 --> 00:34:20.570
(general laughter)

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:22.320
And I guess that,

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:25.950
but continue the studies
and deepening the study.

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:29.410
That's why I'm so happy to be
here because it's so important

00:34:29.410 --> 00:34:34.333
that academia takes the
focus out of the agenda

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:38.670
that is a traditional agenda of gender,

00:34:38.670 --> 00:34:40.280
which we need to continue

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:43.227
because human rights is
super important for us,

00:34:43.227 --> 00:34:45.340
and we have to put it at the center,

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:48.524
but we also have to analyze, you know,

00:34:48.524 --> 00:34:51.390
these things that I'm saying about

00:34:51.390 --> 00:34:55.170
getting the autonomy of
women, economic empowerment,

00:34:55.170 --> 00:35:00.170
because I am convinced that
the more autonomy a woman is

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:05.290
the less likely she will
be a victim of violence.

00:35:05.750 --> 00:35:08.900
So I think that things are related,

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:10.530
and also she will have

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:13.780
the leadership skills as
well to become a politician.

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:18.553
So, I mean, I tend to be
romantic and believe in all that.

00:35:19.712 --> 00:35:21.860
- It's, is good to be romantic in,

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:26.420
especially because we need
people who are convinced of this.

00:35:26.420 --> 00:35:29.160
And I think I cannot
think of better colleagues

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:31.687
than UN Women to pass this passion.

00:35:31.687 --> 00:35:34.580
I want to ask you a question of my own

00:35:34.580 --> 00:35:37.510
while, you know, students
think of other questions.

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:40.750
You mentioned about the importance of

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:45.395
investing in women's projects.

00:35:45.395 --> 00:35:50.220
We have some students from
business schools, right?

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:52.053
And of course at the end of the day,

00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:54.670
not, nobody's going to invest

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:56.800
just because they are women's project,

00:35:56.800 --> 00:35:59.831
they need to be, as you
said, good business.

00:35:59.831 --> 00:36:00.664
- Yes, yes.

00:36:00.664 --> 00:36:03.340
- So tell us a little bit more
about this funds that you,

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:06.461
that you're proposing as

00:36:06.461 --> 00:36:09.517
especially to the venture
capitalist, to the new.

00:36:09.517 --> 00:36:11.410
- So I'm not proposing a fund,

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:14.570
I'm proposing a platform of knowledge,

00:36:14.570 --> 00:36:18.140
because I remember the
first time we invited

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:22.572
JP Morgan, and Citibank,

00:36:22.572 --> 00:36:23.860
and there was a lot of American banks,

00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:25.270
but also European banks.

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:30.270
And we did it with the University
of Villapressa, Espania,

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:35.080
Spain, with Susana Malcorra,
that used to be the

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:39.650
Chief of Staff of Ban Ki-moon,
the former secretary-general,

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:42.330
and she was also Minister
for the Affairs of Argentina,

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:43.787
Susanna called me and said,

00:36:43.787 --> 00:36:46.620
"How can we work together in this?"

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:49.080
So we decided to create this platform

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:51.120
together with the European Union,

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:54.230
the European Bank of Reconstruction,

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:57.080
but we were talking about

00:36:57.080 --> 00:37:02.080
investments that yield
very positive results,

00:37:02.600 --> 00:37:04.938
but are products for women

00:37:04.938 --> 00:37:09.938
women's lead organizations
or entrepreneurs,

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:12.390
and also services for women.

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:13.590
And I give you an example.

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:17.820
And there, I found by researching

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:20.020
who is who in the financial world,

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:22.950
and why is it that they're
not investing in women?

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:27.840
And I came across with the
first unicorn of the region

00:37:29.275 --> 00:37:32.900
and she was called
herself the pink unicorn.

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:34.670
She's little like this,

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:37.570
she's from Argentina,
and she lives in Miami.

00:37:37.570 --> 00:37:39.037
So I went to see her and said,

00:37:39.037 --> 00:37:40.260
"How did you manage,

00:37:40.260 --> 00:37:44.750
to get $1 billion for women's investment?"

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:47.980
And she said, "It was very difficult.

00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:52.980
I had to navigate a lot
of sexual harassment.

00:37:53.980 --> 00:37:58.880
I had to navigate a lot
of, sexual," conversation?

00:37:58.880 --> 00:37:59.713
- Bias.

00:37:59.713 --> 00:38:04.440
- "Bias from men that
they just wanted to have

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:08.270
dinner or lunch with me,
just for the sake of it.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:11.380
And it took only one man,

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:14.420
that was a big investor, that realized,

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:17.630
'I hear you. I will invest in you.'

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:22.630
And when he decided, then
it came across many others."

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:24.660
And one of the investments

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:26.690
that was the first investment that,

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:30.390
that she came across was an American,

00:38:30.390 --> 00:38:34.260
biotechnology, that she invented,

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:37.210
and most women are gonna
sympathize with me,

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:42.210
a machine to do the mammography,
but with a gender lens.

00:38:42.670 --> 00:38:47.670
It's, is a machine that
captures your, your breast,

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:51.560
and you don't have to be squeeze as it is,

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:53.223
which is horrible the way it is,

00:38:53.223 --> 00:38:55.787
because of course it was done by a man

00:38:55.787 --> 00:38:56.620
(general laughter)

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.650
because no women can really
invent a machine like that.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:03.200
So she decided she decided to create this,

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:06.700
this machine, and also it's mobile,

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:10.810
so it means that you can go
to rural areas and et cetera.

00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:12.765
So, you know,

00:39:12.765 --> 00:39:15.770
she came with this
investment of $20 million

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:20.770
and this man realized that
this is a money making machine.

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:25.060
So he invested a hundred million dollars.

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:26.347
So the American lady said,

00:39:26.347 --> 00:39:30.170
"Oh my God, what I'm gonna do
now with this kind of money?"

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:33.810
So now he, she's producing the machines

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:38.100
and most likely you
will benefit from that.

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:41.680
But this is an example of
a product and a service

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:44.830
that is done with a gender lens.

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:47.240
And it took one man to realize

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:49.730
that we really need to achieve that.

00:39:49.730 --> 00:39:51.160
So that's what we are trying to do;

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:53.800
to educate the financial sector,

00:39:53.800 --> 00:39:56.380
to understand that they are biases.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:59.650
And we need a, a test with the IDB,

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:02.920
the Inter-American Development
Bank Research Department.

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:07.920
We send 20 actors and
actresses, 10 men and 10 women,

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:11.910
in Santiago, Chile, to
get a loan from a bank.

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:13.860
The same conditions, everything,

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:16.140
they were young, they were 28

00:40:17.818 --> 00:40:22.730
and 20% of women were rejected, the loans,

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:24.600
just because they were women.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:29.600
And then we realized that the
risk analysts were 99% men.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:33.287
And the bias was,

00:40:33.287 --> 00:40:36.920
"Oh, you are at the age of conception.

00:40:36.920 --> 00:40:39.000
Most likely you will be a mother,

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.160
so you will not pay me the loan."

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:41.993
Why?

00:40:41.993 --> 00:40:46.993
I mean, 99% of the return of debt

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:50.490
women are, are 99% responsible,

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:53.390
much more than men that are 80%.

00:40:53.390 --> 00:40:57.740
So these bias, we needed to
explain, we needed to educate.

00:40:57.740 --> 00:41:00.660
And of course, when we went to,

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:02.710
we had a meeting with the
presidents of the banks,

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:07.667
they were all men, you know,
and when I presented, I said,

00:41:07.667 --> 00:41:10.714
"You lost 6 billion dollars of business

00:41:10.714 --> 00:41:12.597
because of the bias."

00:41:12.597 --> 00:41:13.721
"What!" they said.

00:41:13.721 --> 00:41:14.600
(general laughter)

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:15.510
There you go.

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:17.140
When you have bias,

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:19.110
when you don't understand

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:22.433
the product and service
that women wants to do,

00:41:23.449 --> 00:41:27.240
and the fact that women are most likely

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:32.240
more inclined to work as a
network, the sorority of women,

00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:36.000
the association compared
to, you know, you name it.

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:38.580
We could be a force of nature.

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:42.030
The energy that women of this region has,

00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:46.220
we can be outta poverty very soon.

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:48.440
So this is what we are fighting.

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:53.150
You know, the patriarchy, this bias, this,

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:56.900
these ways of not
understanding the diversity,

00:41:56.900 --> 00:42:01.135
how the diversity can help
for the business environment.

00:42:01.135 --> 00:42:05.210
So this is what we are trying
to do with this platform.

00:42:05.210 --> 00:42:08.430
- Great story María-Noel, the great story.

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:12.493
Any more questions from the
audience, please, we have.

00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:15.820
- My name is Mariana.

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:17.535
I'm from School of the Environment,

00:42:17.535 --> 00:42:19.680
I'm graduating this year as well,

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:22.390
and I was wondering if within UN Women,

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:25.799
there have been talks about
how to create cultural change,

00:42:25.799 --> 00:42:30.220
maybe at the local levels,
in schools, because I think

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:32.660
one of the biggest barriers
for women's empowerment

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:34.130
and women equality in the region

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:36.800
is even when women have the opportunity,

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:39.250
they weren't raised to feel like they,

00:42:39.250 --> 00:42:41.250
they were capable of taking those roles

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:43.110
and the men around them

00:42:43.110 --> 00:42:45.480
are not encouraging them
to take those roles.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.030
So how, how do we address that?

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:49.440
And in a way that's
respectful to, of course,

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:52.200
to the region and, and
to the current norms,

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:56.310
but building towards a
more equal society for all?

00:42:56.310 --> 00:42:58.060
- Thank you for the question.

00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:01.550
We are doing it by three things.

00:43:01.550 --> 00:43:04.290
We work a lot with UNESCO and with UNICEF,

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:06.280
as well as other UN agencies

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:09.170
that are more in education than us.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:11.846
We did a pro, we have a
program that is called

00:43:11.846 --> 00:43:16.846
Second Chance Education,
in Mexico and in Chile,

00:43:16.960 --> 00:43:20.960
those are pilot projects,
financed by a private foundation.

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:24.730
And what we are doing there
is to reach out to those women

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:27.760
that left the education system,

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:29.170
because of what you're saying,

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:32.990
because they were early
mothers, early pregnancies,

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:37.880
and because they were pushed
by their partners to stay home

00:43:38.927 --> 00:43:42.180
and because they didn't
have the self-confidence.

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:45.340
So this program is about self-confidence,

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:50.250
it's about rebuilding
the willpower of women,

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:52.490
and most likely those women

00:43:52.490 --> 00:43:55.563
are victims of psychological
violence, right?

00:43:56.726 --> 00:44:00.710
So we have 2000 women in
Mexico, not in Mexico City,

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:04.470
but in two states, and
what I saw was incredible

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:06.650
because I met them the first day

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:11.050
that we started the program,
nobody dared to talk.

00:44:11.050 --> 00:44:13.210
They were crying all the time.

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:16.550
You know, they were, you
know, they were nervous,

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:18.330
and with fear,

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:21.550
what is gonna happen if my
husband sees that I'm here?

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:26.520
And then two years later, I
came to visit the program.

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:28.770
I couldn't believe what I saw, you know,

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:31.187
those women with self-confidence.

00:44:31.187 --> 00:44:32.990
"I am an entrepreneur.

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:34.820
I handle the money now.

00:44:34.820 --> 00:44:37.141
If he doesn't like it,
he can leave," you know,

00:44:37.141 --> 00:44:38.175
(general laughter)

00:44:38.175 --> 00:44:40.450
and I say, "Oh my God,
what happened here?"

00:44:40.450 --> 00:44:43.801
So by building their self-esteem,

00:44:43.801 --> 00:44:46.680
and it's not traditional
education that you want,

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:49.180
but it's the education they need.

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:53.830
And in the pandemic, most of
them lost their entrepreneur,

00:44:53.830 --> 00:44:58.830
you know, initiatives,
but they rebuilt others.

00:44:58.940 --> 00:45:01.610
And the most important thing that I saw

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:04.310
was the network between indigenous women,

00:45:04.310 --> 00:45:08.460
between women that were

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:11.830
discriminated because of their age,

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:15.210
because they were too
young or too old, you know?

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:20.200
So we have to tackle the self-confidence

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:25.200
because that's why it's
been too many centuries

00:45:25.200 --> 00:45:28.620
of telling, "You can't do
it because you're a woman.

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:30.700
You can't do it because you're a woman."

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:33.510
So they are, "It's built in your genes!

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:36.700
You can't do it because
you're a woman," you know?

00:45:36.700 --> 00:45:40.820
So we have to remake it and
we have to understand that

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:44.440
self-confidence will
take you very far away

00:45:44.440 --> 00:45:49.440
and will allow you to be
brilliant in whatever you do.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:55.140
And I think we are still
in the era of first time,

00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:59.150
first time, an astronaut
women that goes to the moon.

00:45:59.150 --> 00:46:01.970
First time a woman becomes a unicorn.

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:04.470
First time a woman is president.

00:46:04.470 --> 00:46:08.400
So we are still in that era, you know,

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:12.610
so what we need is accelerators.

00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:15.630
We need the accelerators
by changing the behavior.

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:17.360
And, to answer your question,

00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:20.250
we are using behavioral science.

00:46:20.250 --> 00:46:23.550
We are using positive deviance.

00:46:23.550 --> 00:46:28.550
We are trying to bring all of the menu of,

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:32.563
of things that are happening
in innovation, not just,

00:46:34.360 --> 00:46:38.360
but I think behavioral
science, neu, neuroscience,

00:46:38.360 --> 00:46:41.600
it's important to use
it to change community.

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:44.150
And also, and that, that's my last point,

00:46:44.150 --> 00:46:46.670
we are working with religious leaders

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:51.061
because religion is part of our life.

00:46:51.061 --> 00:46:54.330
And, you know, I realized

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:56.420
by working with religious leaders

00:46:56.420 --> 00:46:59.930
of all different religious,

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:03.780
they didn't even know they were
doing their discrimination,

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:06.570
or they didn't even realize

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:09.720
how much harm they were doing to women.

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:14.720
So it was so interesting to
work with Catholic priests,

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:19.770
pastors from different
denominations, Muslims.

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:21.463
We have an enormous network that,

00:47:21.463 --> 00:47:23.500
that we work on a regular basis.

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:25.150
And, you know,

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:28.360
they thought that feminist
was about abortion,

00:47:28.360 --> 00:47:32.130
or was about, you know,
shouting in the streets

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:35.050
so they didn't realize all these things.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:36.327
And they say,

00:47:36.327 --> 00:47:37.670
"You are right.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:40.670
We need to respect the dignity of women.

00:47:40.670 --> 00:47:44.500
That is every religion,
the dignity of women."

00:47:44.500 --> 00:47:47.900
So that's what we are,
and that's why for us

00:47:47.900 --> 00:47:51.310
human rights of women are essential

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:53.653
and central to our work.

00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:57.700
- I would say, María-Noel,
this is so important,

00:47:57.700 --> 00:47:58.657
what you just said.

00:47:58.657 --> 00:48:02.440
And of course we're
counting on Yale students.

00:48:02.440 --> 00:48:03.273
- Yes.

00:48:03.273 --> 00:48:06.520
- Male and female, we're
going to become world leaders.

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:10.145
We're going to get the jobs
to also think about this,

00:48:10.145 --> 00:48:12.830
in advance, because it also,

00:48:12.830 --> 00:48:17.830
this self-esteem for women
it also is part of being,

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:21.853
looking at role models,
like many of you will be,

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:24.950
and school other women.

00:48:24.950 --> 00:48:27.840
And, you know, you're gonna
be working in your countries.

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:29.980
Many of you are from our region.

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:31.530
So I, we are counting on them.

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:32.520
- Yes.

00:48:32.520 --> 00:48:36.890
- So I think we have one
more question from Juan,

00:48:36.890 --> 00:48:37.900
- the gentleman,

00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:39.160
- Juan.

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:39.993
- Thank you so much.

00:48:39.993 --> 00:48:41.750
I'm Juan, first year Master's student

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:45.180
in Jackson Institute for
Global Affairs from Colombia

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:47.153
and as well have many questions,

00:48:47.153 --> 00:48:50.118
some of which we have
like started to answer,

00:48:50.118 --> 00:48:52.280
but I guess my main one is

00:48:52.280 --> 00:48:55.390
how does the challenges of
working to empower women

00:48:55.390 --> 00:48:58.370
between rural and urban areas changes

00:48:58.370 --> 00:49:00.820
because it's still kind of
an urban movement and the

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:04.200
rural ones are even like
in a worse condition,

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:06.720
so how, what are the
difference in the challenges

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:09.530
and how are you like
creating different strategies

00:49:09.530 --> 00:49:10.540
to make sure that also like

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:13.280
rural women are empowered properly?

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:14.650
- Thank you for your question.

00:49:14.650 --> 00:49:17.330
And yes, it is different,

00:49:17.330 --> 00:49:22.280
the objectives in life, but
is also different the context

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:25.500
and the context for equality and diversity

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:27.053
is very important as well.

00:49:28.040 --> 00:49:31.460
I would say that in
Colombia, in particular,

00:49:31.460 --> 00:49:33.040
after the peace agreements,

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:36.240
we have a hundred provisions
of gender equality

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:37.350
in the peace agreement.

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:39.720
So we are working with women

00:49:39.720 --> 00:49:43.290
that were most affected by the conflict.

00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:47.320
So, for us, it has been
an amazing opportunity

00:49:47.320 --> 00:49:52.320
to realize the leadership that
is emerging in rural areas,

00:49:52.670 --> 00:49:55.630
indigenous women, Afro-descendant women.

00:49:55.630 --> 00:50:00.080
And their voice will never be shut down.

00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:04.460
I can tell you once they started,
they are amazing leaders.

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:08.660
And we have the opportunity,
perhaps we don't know yet,

00:50:08.660 --> 00:50:13.130
but there is a very prominent
Afro-descendant leader

00:50:13.130 --> 00:50:15.580
that is candidate to be vice president.

00:50:15.580 --> 00:50:18.920
And this is the first time that
is happening in the country.

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:22.040
So I hope that, you know,

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:27.040
the rural women have the same
needs as the urban women,

00:50:27.530 --> 00:50:31.400
but they've been treated differently.

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:34.540
In the self-esteem, they
need the same things,

00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:37.330
in the political participation as well,

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:39.480
and the economic empowerment as well.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:42.113
And they suffer violence the same.

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:47.940
I think is the intensity and
it's the lack of networks,

00:50:47.940 --> 00:50:51.440
and so that's why we need
to push for more networks.

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:56.440
And we need to be more aware,
from the UN in particular,

00:50:56.570 --> 00:50:59.490
to these amazing women that
are emerging as leaders

00:50:59.490 --> 00:51:01.620
and bring them to the table.

00:51:01.620 --> 00:51:04.760
Normally we tend to work
with the urban leaders

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:06.563
because they are the most noisy,

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:12.640
but we need to be caring for
the one that are left behind.

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:16.450
And I see in the region, it's emerging,

00:51:16.450 --> 00:51:20.390
a lot of indigenous women,
Afro-descendant women.

00:51:20.390 --> 00:51:21.960
We had an amazing leader

00:51:21.960 --> 00:51:24.470
in the vice president of Costa Rica.

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:27.260
She's Afro-descendant and in Costa Rica,

00:51:27.260 --> 00:51:31.870
you never think that there
is Afro-descendant population

00:51:31.870 --> 00:51:35.333
that is a minority, but
is extremely important.

00:51:36.461 --> 00:51:39.360
And I think that the more we have,

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:42.320
the more we have to integrate them

00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:45.650
and, you know, provide
them with opportunities

00:51:45.650 --> 00:51:49.470
of more training or more exposure.

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:54.310
We went to the security council

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:59.310
with the mothers of the Sepur Zarco.

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:05.160
It was a location in
Guatemala where the women were

00:52:05.280 --> 00:52:09.390
systematically raped as
an instrument of war.

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:10.840
So we demonstrated,

00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:13.040
this is the only case in the world

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:17.780
that by weaponizing the sexual slavery,

00:52:20.430 --> 00:52:24.320
the national law condemned the militaries,

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:25.580
it's the first.

00:52:25.580 --> 00:52:29.670
So we went to the Security, and
it was only indigenous women

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:31.520
who were telling their story,

00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:35.430
in their own language because
they didn't speak Spanish.

00:52:35.430 --> 00:52:40.430
So, you know, we are
trying to make role models,

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:44.630
to bring them to the tables

00:52:44.630 --> 00:52:47.380
and to the conversations.

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:49.200
You know, that with the pandemic,

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:52.210
we were all the time in Zoom, you know,

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:56.280
but they didn't have technology,
so they didn't have access.

00:52:56.280 --> 00:53:00.040
40% of the women in Latin America
they don't have computers,

00:53:00.040 --> 00:53:02.000
they don't have access to Internet.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:04.950
40% and we are talking about, you know,

00:53:04.950 --> 00:53:06.993
40% of 300 million.

00:53:07.960 --> 00:53:12.340
So, you know, by trying
to listening to them

00:53:12.340 --> 00:53:15.220
because they are solution makers

00:53:15.220 --> 00:53:17.560
for climate changes adaptation,

00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:21.710
climate changes mitigation,
to preserve the biodiversity.

00:53:21.710 --> 00:53:25.860
So it is so, so important
to listen to them

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:28.550
and integrate their demands in the,

00:53:28.550 --> 00:53:32.240
and also the feminist
movement is very particular

00:53:32.240 --> 00:53:35.040
because they don't like young.

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:37.317
They don't like the
young because they said,

00:53:37.317 --> 00:53:39.360
"They don't know, they
don't have experience."

00:53:39.360 --> 00:53:41.940
Hello. What are you talking about?

00:53:41.940 --> 00:53:46.940
So we had a lot of problems
with a traditional feminists.

00:53:47.330 --> 00:53:50.877
They didn't want it,
the younger generation.

00:53:50.877 --> 00:53:52.280
"They don't have an NGO.

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:54.680
They are movements. They will disappear."

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:55.570
No.

00:53:55.570 --> 00:53:59.610
So I have an advisory board
as a Regional Director

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:01.910
and they are so diverse.

00:54:01.910 --> 00:54:04.380
This is the first time
they are very diverse

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:07.650
because I brought young, from every,

00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:10.250
every intersectionality

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:12.557
and the traditional families say,

00:54:12.557 --> 00:54:14.777
"How come I'm not in your board?"

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:19.310
You will never be, (general laughter)

00:54:19.310 --> 00:54:21.520
because you don't bring the diversity.

00:54:21.520 --> 00:54:24.060
And I need to listen to what are the,

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:26.170
and also I brought one man.

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:28.107
So everybody was indignated.

00:54:28.107 --> 00:54:29.310
"How can you bring a man?"

00:54:29.310 --> 00:54:33.580
Because we, "They're the solution
to our problems as well."

00:54:33.580 --> 00:54:37.260
So I think that we need to
listen to each other much more.

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:39.570
We need to work much more with men

00:54:40.763 --> 00:54:43.540
and we need to bring
leaders that are respectful

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:47.470
of the dignity of women no
matter where we come from.

00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:50.170
The intersectionality is critical

00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:53.850
and human rights are still, you know,

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:58.150
the values that, that we need to defend.

00:54:58.150 --> 00:55:02.270
Otherwise, I am not very optimistic

00:55:02.270 --> 00:55:07.160
of what is happening in the
region from political side,

00:55:07.160 --> 00:55:11.140
but I am very optimistic
with the youth of the region

00:55:11.140 --> 00:55:13.273
and of course the women of the region.

00:55:14.450 --> 00:55:19.400
- Thank you very much, María-Noel,
for this wonderful talk

00:55:19.400 --> 00:55:21.940
of so many important issues
that you have brought.

00:55:21.940 --> 00:55:26.830
And especially for showing
here at Yale, how broad,

00:55:26.830 --> 00:55:30.570
and how new the gender
and diversity agenda is

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:32.240
and so many topics

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:37.240
that are still on the
table that need to evolve.

00:55:37.780 --> 00:55:41.770
I would like, we have literally
two minutes before we,

00:55:41.770 --> 00:55:42.603
we cut.

00:55:42.603 --> 00:55:45.243
So I would like to ask you,

00:55:45.243 --> 00:55:49.870
a final question is, your recommendations

00:55:49.870 --> 00:55:53.000
that you would like to
leave the Yale students.

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:54.440
- Yes.

00:55:54.440 --> 00:55:56.760
- So many, as you can
tell, they are interested

00:55:56.760 --> 00:55:59.490
and I have seen them in
my classes, how engaged,

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:00.883
how interested they are,

00:56:01.790 --> 00:56:06.340
how can they contribute to
advance gender and diversity,

00:56:06.340 --> 00:56:09.494
not only in our region, in
Latin America and the Caribbean,

00:56:09.494 --> 00:56:12.700
but in their own, which
where they're gonna

00:56:12.700 --> 00:56:16.090
be moving and working going forward?

00:56:16.090 --> 00:56:19.100
- Well, first of all, you are
the leaders of the future,

00:56:19.100 --> 00:56:20.850
as Jessica was saying

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:24.300
and I think that you will be

00:56:25.500 --> 00:56:28.930
much broader leaders

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:32.200
if you understand the gender dimension

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:35.180
in all the aspects that
we were discussing.

00:56:35.180 --> 00:56:40.180
I believe that Yale, as a
fantastic institution it is,

00:56:42.200 --> 00:56:47.200
if you do more research on
these matters, if you put your,

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:51.640
you know, your focus on researching these

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:53.670
alternative issues of gender,

00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:57.720
because normally academic
focus on human rights,

00:56:57.720 --> 00:57:01.740
on public policies, that needs to be done,

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:05.060
that, I would say that, sometimes

00:57:05.060 --> 00:57:10.060
the research does not match
what is happening in the region.

00:57:10.610 --> 00:57:13.910
So it would be great if
we can have new students

00:57:13.910 --> 00:57:15.800
to come more to the region

00:57:15.800 --> 00:57:20.800
and to bring the amazing
academic structures

00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:24.990
that you are receiving
here to Latin America,

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:29.827
because we still have
very poor universities,

00:57:30.730 --> 00:57:35.030
poor in the sense that
we are not modernized.

00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:37.420
I see when I talk with students

00:57:37.420 --> 00:57:42.420
that they think in a unilateral way.

00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:45.910
They are not crossing different frontiers.

00:57:45.910 --> 00:57:47.960
And I think that we need to do

00:57:47.960 --> 00:57:51.270
much more cross-fertilization work.

00:57:51.270 --> 00:57:54.500
Yesterday I have the
privilege to have dinner with

00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:57.540
fellow students from Turkey, from Israel,

00:57:57.540 --> 00:57:59.510
from Mongolia, from Germany,

00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:02.400
and they are doing amazing work.

00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:05.270
I was so excited to listen to them.

00:58:05.270 --> 00:58:07.910
Thank you very much, for you to,

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:11.460
oh, she's doing our research
on an amazing issue.

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:16.450
Why is it that women in
Turkey are voting conservative

00:58:16.450 --> 00:58:19.390
when the conservative
government is pushing women

00:58:19.390 --> 00:58:21.350
to be much more conservative?

00:58:21.350 --> 00:58:22.660
So there, there is,

00:58:22.660 --> 00:58:26.690
there are so many issues
that you guys can have the,

00:58:26.690 --> 00:58:28.330
the luxury to research,

00:58:28.330 --> 00:58:31.260
being in a, such a
prestigious institution.

00:58:31.260 --> 00:58:34.570
So I really would like
to see much more research

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:36.950
being done on these issues,

00:58:36.950 --> 00:58:40.670
cross-fertilized on different
regions of the world,

00:58:40.670 --> 00:58:43.800
because we are called
the middle-income region,

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:47.090
but we are the most unequal
region in the world.

00:58:47.090 --> 00:58:49.840
And we are still with low productivity,

00:58:49.840 --> 00:58:52.080
with so much inequality,

00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:56.550
how can we get out of this
trap that we are a as a region?

00:58:56.550 --> 00:59:01.550
So economic factor, financial
factors, climate changes.

00:59:01.570 --> 00:59:05.560
So all these factors, it's
not just gender issues,

00:59:05.560 --> 00:59:10.140
it's like how the perspective
of women can be studied there.

00:59:10.140 --> 00:59:13.540
And, for me also, it would be an honor,

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:18.540
if we can have some of
youth come into UN Women,

00:59:18.610 --> 00:59:21.322
we have offices in many countries,

00:59:21.322 --> 00:59:23.270
our regional office is in Panama,

00:59:23.270 --> 00:59:26.450
but we have offices in, our
biggest office is in Colombia.

00:59:26.450 --> 00:59:29.170
And then we have in
Haiti, in the Caribbean,

00:59:29.170 --> 00:59:32.110
in Mexico, Argentina, you name it, Brazil.

00:59:32.110 --> 00:59:34.990
So you are much welcome

00:59:34.990 --> 00:59:39.412
to come to our offices to
do your research in situ

00:59:39.412 --> 00:59:42.040
and that would be great.

00:59:42.040 --> 00:59:45.710
So thank you so much for
giving me the opportunity.

00:59:45.710 --> 00:59:48.153
And I hope that I put some seed of,

00:59:49.240 --> 00:59:52.890
of trying to continue
working in gender issues

00:59:52.890 --> 00:59:54.505
but in different dimensions.

00:59:54.505 --> 00:59:55.338
Thank you.

00:59:55.338 --> 00:59:56.497
- Thank you so much for coming,

00:59:56.497 --> 00:59:57.501
(audience applauds)

00:59:57.501 --> 00:59:59.575
(indistinct)

00:59:59.575 --> 01:00:03.091
(applause continues)

01:00:03.091 --> 01:00:06.258
(music chimes softly)

